WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.424
A buffer is not just a pointer.

2
00:00:02.424 --> 00:00:07.271
It has storage, capacity, current length, element type, lifetime, ownership, and valid access rules.

3
00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:11.425
Safe buffer design keeps those facts visible for as long as possible.

4
00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:16.619
When they are separated, mistakes become much easier to write and much harder to review.

5
00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:21.812
Raw pointers and C-style arrays often lose important context when passed across function boundaries.

6
00:00:21.812 --> 00:00:30.814
A callee may know where data starts, but not how much storage exists, how much data is initialized, who owns the storage, or whether the pointer

7
00:00:30.814 --> 00:00:32.891
points to one element or many.

8
00:00:32.891 --> 00:00:35.661
That missing context is where boundary mistakes grow.

9
00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:39.123
Null termination is not the same as a verified length.

10
00:00:39.123 --> 00:00:44.663
A C-style string convention depends on a terminator being present before the valid storage ends.

11
00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:50.202
If code assumes termination without knowing the buffer capacity, it may read beyond the intended range.

12
00:00:50.202 --> 00:00:53.319
Text interfaces should define length and encoding expectations clearly.

13
00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:56.435
Byte buffers and character strings require different handling rules.

14
00:00:56.435 --> 00:01:00.935
Binary data may contain zero bytes and should not be treated as text.

15
00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:03.359
Encoded text may change length when converted.

16
00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:09.591
A destination that is large enough for characters in one representation may not be large enough after transformation.

17
00:01:09.591 --> 00:01:12.361
Safe APIs preserve ownership and bounds across boundaries.

18
00:01:12.361 --> 00:01:17.554
Containers, spans, string types, buffer objects, and explicit length parameters all help maintain the contract.

19
00:01:17.554 --> 00:01:23.440
The goal is to make valid access rules obvious to the caller, the callee, tests, and reviewers.
